speechtext:
We have been told by the Minister of Pub-lie Works that they were sold to the extent of thirty or forty million dollars. Certain it is though, by all the evidence that could be adduced before that committee, that there were certain bonds issued but never sold until after certain decisions were given. I will admit that the bonds were sold, but under what conditions were they sold? They were sold under these conditions-and this is where I say the bondholder is not entitled to one jot or tittle of sympathy. What did he do? No bondholder or set of bondholders will take up several million dollars' worth of bonds without going to the bottom of the whole affair and ascertaining whereon the authority of the company to issue the bonds rests. Very properly they found out, and what did the bondholders say? This conversation occurred as surely as the night follows the day. They said: Show us your charter; show us the proceedings upon which your charter was founded. And they did. They knew of the litigation and knew that there was a doubt whether or not they had the right to exercise the rights that the court upheld. They recognized the fact, as we do here to-night, that the rights given to this company were purely a piece of judicial legislation which has always taken place, and which judicial legislation is nine times out of ten not in the minds of those who pass it. They said:	We will	take your	bonds;
but they did not take them without the guarantee of the four companies. They said: We will take these bonds provided the four companies-the Electrical Development Company, the Toronto Street Railway, the Toronto and Niagara. Power Company and the Toronto Electric Light Company- guarantee them. And they did. Are the bondholders hurt? The hon. Minister of Public Works said: If you pass this legislation to right a wrong that was never intended by this Parliament these bonds will not be worth fifty cents in the dollar. With the guarantee that these bondholders hold, they are worth dollar for dollar, and the company and the bondholders know it right well. Therefore I think I might ask in all fairness, why should we by our vote give to this company the right to walk into any municipality in the Dominion and say: We may go here, there and everywhere, and the wishes or the rights of this municipality amount to absolutely nothing as against the powers that Parliament gave to us inadvertently, but which we intend to take advantage of. I say, Sir, that if you have not 
local autonomy in your Dominion, provincial and municipal affairs, and if we here, the people of the Dominion of Canada in our various spheres, are not allowed to exercise that autonomy in the way that seems best to us as communities, then the sooner we make up our minds to hand over the Dominion of Canada to companies who may desire to do as they please with us without any reference whatever to the iwill of the people, the better. It is a plain statement of facts. There has been much camouflage, and a great many red herrings have been drawn across the trail, but the matter is plain. And when I listened to the Hon. James Beck the other day in this Chamber 1 thought that as a speech, and in touching only the high spots, it was an absolute classic. He stood before the members of that committee and I will admit he played upon their feelings, and he played upon mine. But the one burden of that speech, and the sole tune of that song was: Take care, oh do take care of the down-trodden bondholder. There is no down-trodden bondholder, and there is no repudiation of the contract of this Parliament in the slightest degree. That charter was granted on a distinct understanding, the persons who got it well knowing that their powers were confined within certain terms. They went on and completed their works, and as my hon. friend from Pontiac (Mr. Cahill) so well said, they were supplying only two customers, and knew that the lives of those two customers were limited. Why, as prudent business men, did they not establish a sinking fund? They did not do so, but traded upon a judicial interpretation of statutes never intended, and consequently find themselves in the cul de sac in which they are to-day. So far as I am personally concerned, I am here representing the people and not the corporations, and I believe that this is one of the times when the House of Commons should stand absolutely by the rights of the people without fear or favour.
